## Conclusions
We have reported a continuous-flow spray-drying method for aqueous production of two representative $Z r$ -MOFs: UiO-66- $N H_{2}$ and $Z r$ -fumarate. For both $Z r$ -MOFs, a modulator (acetic acid) concentration of $30\%$ (v/v in water) was found to be optimum, as it yielded microbeads whose $S_{\mathsf{B E T}}$ and wateruptake values were comparable to literature values obtained using other methods. In addition, our method obviates postsynthetic shaping methods, which usually reduce the porosity of MOFs. Lastly, as proof-of-concept on the scalability of our spray-drying approach, we used it to fabricate several grams of ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ beads. Thus, this work opens the possibility of using spray-drying, a mature process already integrated in many industrial sectors, as a green method for the continuous one-step fabrication of shaped MOF microbeads.
## Experimental section
## Materials and methods
Zirconium oxychloride octahydrate, 2-aminoterephthalic acid, fumaric acid and acetic acid were purchased from SigmaAldrich. All reagents were used without further purification. All reactions were performed using deionised water, obtained from a Milli-Q system (18.2 MΩcm).
X-ray powder diffraction patterns were collected on an X'Pert PRO MPDP analytical diffractometer (Panalytical) at 40 kV and $40~\mathsf{m A}$ using $\mathsf{C u K a}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5419\mathrm{~\AA})$ . Nitrogen adsorption and desorption measurements were performed at 77 K using an Autosorb-IQ-AG analyser (Quantachrome Instruments). Specific surface area $(S_{\mathsf{B E T}})$ was determined by applying the BET equation to the adequate region of the nitrogen isotherms. Pore volume was determined from the adsorbed volume at $P/P_{0}\ 0.3$ of each isotherm. Prior to the measurements, samples were degassed inside the cell under primary vacuum at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for 6 h $(\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ or at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 12 h (Zr-fumarate). Gravimetric water vapour-sorption isotherms were measured using a DVS vacuum instrument (Surface Measurement Systems Ltd). The weight of the dried powder $(\approx20~\mathsf{m g})$ was constantly monitored with a highresolution microbalance $(\pm0.1\upmu\mathrm{g})$ and recorded at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C\left(\pm\:0.2\right.$ $^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ under pure water vapour pressures. Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) images were collected on a FEI Magellan $400\mathrm{~L~}$ scanning electron microscope at an acceleration voltage of $2.0~\mathsf{k V},$ , and on a FEI Quanta 650F scanning electron microscope at an acceleration voltage of $20.0{\sf K V}$ , using aluminium as a support. The degree of insertion of acetate inside the $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ -fumarate frameworks was analysed by first digesting the samples, and then studying the resulting solutions by $^1\mathsf{H}$ -NMR. For ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ , the acetate $S_{B D C-N H_{2}}$ molar ratio was calculated by comparison of the integration of doublet at 7.75 ppm corresponding to ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}.$ - BDC and singlet at 1.9 ppm corresponding to acetate. In the case of $z_{\r}$ -fumarate, the acetate/fumarate molar ratio was calculated by comparison of the integration of a singlet at 6.64 ppm corresponding to fumarate and a singlet at 1.9 ppm corresponding to acetate. $^1\mathsf{H}$ -NMR spectra of were collected on a Bruker AVANCE III 400 MHz spectrometer. Thermogravimetric analyses were performed on a Perkin Elmer TGA 8000 Thermogravimetric analyser. A heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ was used from room temperature up to $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
## Optimised aqueous synthesis of UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ by continuous-flow spray-drying
$Z r0C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O$ $(773~\mathrm{mg},2.4~\mathrm{mmol})$ and 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(435~\mathrm{mg},2.4~\mathrm{mmol})$ were mixed under stirring in $12~\mathsf{m L}$ of a $30\%(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ solution of acetic acid in water. The resulting mixture was injected, with agitation maintained, into a coil-flow reactor (Pyrex; $3\:\mathrm{mm}$ , inner diameter) at a feed rate $(Q_{1\uparrow})$ of 2.4 $\mathsf{m L}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ and a bath temperature $(\boldsymbol{T}_{\mathrm{c}})$ of $90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The pre-heated yellow slurry was then spray-dried at a flow rate $({\cal{Q}}_{\mathrm{{dg}}})$ of 336 $\mathsf{m L\min^{-1}}$ (spray-cap hole diameter: $0.5~\mathsf{m m}_{\iota}^{\prime}$ ) and an inlet temperature $(\overline{{T}}_{\mathrm{in}})$ of $150\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ using a B-290 Mini Spray Dryer (BUCHI Labortechnik). The resulting yellow powder was collected, dispersed in ethanol at room temperature, precipitated by centrifugation three times, left to stand in ethanol overnight, and washed twice with acetone. The solid was dried for $^{12\mathsf{h}}$ at $75^{\circ}C$ in a conventional oven to afford the final product (yield: $64\%$ ).
## Optimised aqueous synthesis of Zr-fumarate by continuous-flow spray-drying
$Z r0C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O$ $\left\langle644\ \mathrm{mg},2.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\right\rangle$ and fumaric acid $232\ \mathrm{mg},2.0$ mmol) were mixed under stirring in $10\ m L$ of a $30\%$ (v/v) solution of acetic acid in water. The resulting mixture was injected into a coil-flow reactor (Pyrex; $3\:\mathrm{mm}$ , inner diameter), with agitation maintained, at a feed rate of $2.4~\mathsf{m L}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ and bath temperature $(\overline{{T_{\mathrm{c}}}})$ of $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The resulting white slurry was spray-dried at a flow rate $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{dg}}$ of $336~\mathrm{mL~min}^{-1}$ and an inlet temperature $(\tau_{\mathrm{in}})$ of $140~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The resulting white powder was collected, dispersed in water at room temperature, left to stand overnight, and precipitated by centrifugation twice. This process was then repeated with ethanol instead of water. The solid was dried for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $75^{\circ}C$ in a conventional oven to afford the final product (yield: $58\%$ ).
